3464 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133466 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133464 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133467 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133464 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133468 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133464 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133469 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133464 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133464 1077 "selection" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133464 1080 #uuid "6ed317bc-f92e-5a12-baca-6065a34610f6" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133465 1081 "81000" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133465 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133465 1084 #uuid "8c246a06-25d0-5abd-9b21-805b50d90f57" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133466 1081 "9000" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133466 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133466 1084 #uuid "9c729933-bc3c-5f80-b290-6bd91b672e25" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133467 1081 "18000" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133467 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133467 1084 #uuid "adb970d5-75ae-5645-8add-5ce63e734bc9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133468 1081 "3000" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133468 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133468 1084 #uuid "ec449f59-a505-5823-ba67-c5cc7d358719" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133469 1081 "27000" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133469 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133469 1084 #uuid "1bc11aa2-99de-5b4a-bf6f-69855bac26ba" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133470 1069 "e-10592" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133470 1071 "![](images/5e/5ef5c74898174a3c6119eac325c3158b227a9cfc967326298f56c14f891b6bea.png)\n\nWhich angle has a measure of $90^{\\circ}$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133470 1072 #uuid "deb87162-187a-5f89-9157-b84b5d8178f7" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133470 1074 "Let's highlight the angle with a measure $90^\\circ$ on our diagram.\n\n![](images/85/859dda507ff6f983a091ecdbf62e01d27230bb210626232388fd344c3e194a8c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the angle $\\angle{COD}$ (or $\\angle{DOC}$) has a measure of $90^\\circ$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133471 1069 "e-10600" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133471 1071 "![](images/f0/f00f3f66717f230fb42264ca5a0873b394ea1d86002f00517062287f8983f236.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of the angle $\\angle{NRP}$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133471 1072 #uuid "5df19497-b07f-57e3-9589-a488291147bf" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133471 1074 "If we trace along the letters of the angle name, from $N$ to $R$ to $P$, we can see the angle $\\angle{NRP}$.\n\n![](images/1a/1a9498bf166c686b8c2e5c3017aeb8625cf36c35d20069f5eb82adcc5c797fa8.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{NRP}=110^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1069 "e-10994" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1071 "Find $5\\det(A)-\\det(B)$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{13} & 4 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 9 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1072 #uuid "bb2795c3-6359-50d8-9531-1e9dfae7a2ce" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{13} & 4 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 9 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n$\\det(B) = 3 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot (-2) = 24$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133473 1069 "e-10995" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133473 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133473 1072 #uuid "dc780837-b763-5ba9-981a-2373afcb524a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133473 1074 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, $\\mid A \\mid = 0$.\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so\n$\\mid B \\mid = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0$.\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, $\\mid C \\mid = 0$.\n\nTherefore, only $\\mid A \\mid$ and $\\mid C \\mid$ are equal to $0$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133474 1069 "e-10996" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133474 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $\\mid A|=0$.\n\n1. $A$ has a zero column\n2. $A$ has proportional rows\n3. $A$ has identical columns" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133474 1072 #uuid "33ca37e5-c598-52a2-861f-2d5bfa6fd571" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133474 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $A$ has proportional rows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133475 1069 "e-1124" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133475 1071 "What is the period of the function shown below?\n\n![](images/07/0780a6b1bd6f6d738c9e80e13757ff24d688728aaf8ac306284761b0e1124764.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133475 1072 #uuid "9a8dfc78-929c-525b-9403-7c1d11d8713f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133475 1074 "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple of $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133476 1069 "e-1125" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133476 1071 "Part of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $2$ is shown below. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\leq x \\leq 4$?\n\n![](images/59/59abf18dc978f48da4bec95bc702141b2459a327f194fda46d64f12c5bc8ffb4.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133476 1072 #uuid "1529ba15-ecf2-5c3f-871d-ab249ef9af34" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133476 1074 "The graph above shows the function in the interval\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nSince this interval has length $2$, and the period of the function is also $2$, this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $2$.\n\n![](images/41/41a583651bf690810335776b9e7b5620e6b6830e1b59ee9d646c4ab429e17de8.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133477 1069 "e-1126" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133477 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Given that $f$ is periodic with period $4$, what is the value of $f(84)$?\n\n![](images/b7/b763649968dc1a117007eba0b7cb0862473aadf702e8c91fc30ac83149445865.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133477 1072 #uuid "6f991ce1-e19a-5324-830a-a17f7ac22848" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133477 1074 "Since the period is $4$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(84) &= f(84 - 4) \\\\\n&= f(84 - 2 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= ⋯ \\\\\n&= f(84 - 20 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= f(4) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133478 1069 "e-1130" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133478 1071 "The number of hours per year that people in a particular country spent on social media can be modeled by the function\n$h(x)= 12x+305$,\nwhere $x$ is the number of years since the year $2000$. What does the constant $305$ mean in this context?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133478 1072 #uuid "78cd7ebe-c912-5723-bca1-fdeb131a852d" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133478 1074 "Notice that by substituting $x=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nh(0) = 12 \\cdot 0 + 305 = 305\n$$\n\nThe value $x=0$ corresponds to the year $2000$. So in $2000$, the people in this country spent $305$ hours per year on social media.\n\nTherefore, the constant term $305$ represents the number of hours per year that people in this country spent on social media in $2000$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133479 1069 "e-1131" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133479 1071 "Alan saves a certain amount of money each month. The total savings he has can be modeled by the function $S(x)$, where $x$ is the number of months since he started saving. How can the following statement be written using the function notation?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"After $5$ months, Alan saved $\\[math]100$ more than he saved after $4$ months.\"*" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133479 1072 #uuid "ebf1e316-214d-5963-98f1-73c00b7001ab" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133479 1074 "$S(x)$ is the amount of money that Alan saved after $x$ months. So,\n\n- $S(5)$ represents the amount of money that Alan saved after $5$ months, and\n- $S(4)$ represents the amount of money that Alan saved after $4$ months.\n\nAfter $5$ months, Alan saved $\\[math]100$ more than he did after $4$ months, so $S(5)$ is $100$ more than $S(4)$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nS(5) = S(4) + 100\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133480 1069 "e-1133" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133480 1071 "The number of people living in Pasadena, California, can be modeled by the function\n$N(t) = 1\\,750t + 138\\,000$,\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the year $2011$. What does the statement $N(3) > N(2)$ mean in this context?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133480 1072 #uuid "530c24e0-a057-55eb-815d-118b6b36108b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133480 1074 "First, we interpret $N(3)$ and $N(2)$ separately:\n\n- $N(3)$ represents the number of people living in Pasadena $3$ years after $2011$, that is, in $2014$.\n- $N(2)$ represents the number of people living in Pasadena $2$ years after $2011$, that is, in $2013$.\n\nTherefore, the statement $N(3) > N(2)$ means the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"More people lived in Pasadena in $2014$ compared to $2013$.\"*" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133481 1069 "e-11348" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133481 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 4x^3 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the line $z = 4-y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133481 1072 #uuid "2d4b2c66-5600-5712-8ec9-3d7325b247c2" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133481 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4x^{3}dx]dA &= ∬_(D)[x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = \\sqrt{y}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sqrt{y})^{4}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)y^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c8712fa0a30ad94f503efe5e080ee89f83eb21f19fba45033bbedeab08ac816.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)y^{2}dA &= \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}[\\int_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dz]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}[y^{2}z]_{z = 0}^{z = 4 - y}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}y^{2}(4 - y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}4y^{2} - y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{4}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{1}{4}y^{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}(64 - 0) - \\frac{1}{4}(256 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133482 1069 "e-11349" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133482 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = \\left\\{(y,z) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133482 1072 #uuid "295afdb2-2e90-5f1a-8830-d3886d99fbad" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133482 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{z}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dx]dA &= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[x]_{x = 0}^{x = z}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)(z - 0)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA &= \\int_{0}^{π/6}\\int_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1 - y}}(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[\\frac{z^{2}}{2}]_{z = 0}^{z = 2\\sqrt{1 - y}}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)((4(1 - y))/(2) - 0)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/6}2\\cos (2y)dy \\\\\n&= [\\sin (2y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/6} \\\\\n&= \\sin (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\sin (0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133483 1069 "e-114" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133483 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2$. According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133483 1072 #uuid "bb373a5d-aa71-58ed-82e3-b74a8f51d144" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133483 1074 "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](images/02/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(6)=11\n$$\n\n, we must have\n$k\\in[3,11]$.\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function,\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nmay have solutions for $k < 3$, but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133484 1069 "e-115" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133484 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2]$. Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133484 1072 #uuid "d048aae2-5773-5da7-ab18-35d41bfbb53a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133484 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2]$.\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1]$. Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133485 1069 "e-11506" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133485 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}} 2y\\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}A$,\ngiven that $D$ is the region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0$, $x= 1$ and the line $z =x$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133485 1072 #uuid "1b09be02-1515-5277-bac6-9289b9dbe371" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133485 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}2ydy]dA &= ∬_(D)[y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = \\sqrt{x}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)xdA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d102b63c3deb8121ea88cdc994b9410cf28f9c3bb29abc5ea4a14d7e1e7c389.png)\n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)xdA &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x}xdzdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x[z]_{z = 0}^{z = x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133486 1069 "e-11507" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133486 1071 "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x$\nwhere $R$ is the type III region defined as\n$R=\\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq z+xz \\right\\}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133486 1072 #uuid "216b16aa-d279-519e-a749-b9fd5517a6db" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133486 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq u_2(x,z) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(x,z)}^{u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d y \\right] \\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(x,z) = 0, \\qquad u_2(x,z) = z+xz\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{S} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{z+xz} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{z + xz}f(x, y, z)dy]dA &= \\int_{-3}^{3}\\int_{x}^{2x + 2}[\\int_{0}^{z + xz}f(x, y, z)dy]dzdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-3}^{3}\\int_{x}^{2x + 2}\\int_{0}^{z + xz}f(x, y, z)dydzdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133487 1069 "e-116" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133487 1071 "On which type of closed intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\,$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133487 1072 #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133487 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0$, which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133488 1069 "e-12369" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133488 1071 "![](images/fc/fc19dd4415663ab027e0fc386d4c5bc81a28c1bc26d5f6cf2083f4f4228a4dea.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133488 1072 #uuid "ad3e88cb-f213-509b-93c5-3fc529d617c0" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133488 1074 "![](images/f2/f25c393f10c35ffd9b4fa91cdf672a516cf6cff974a8aa563d350619a64b79bd.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/14/145f0d494ffe24de84e3a289b3a7316d3f4933f193d734aed118609f7fb9f94f.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint \\limits_{R} 12z^2 \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{3-y-z} 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3 - y - z}12z^{2}dxdzdy &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{3 - y - z}12z^{2}dx]dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}12z^{2}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = 3 - y - z}dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}12z^{2}(3 - y - z)dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dzdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dzdy &= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{1}(36z^{2} - 12yz^{2} - 12z^{3})dz]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[12z^{3} - 4yz^{3} - 3z^{4}]_{z = 0}^{z = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(12 - 4y - 3)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(9 - 4y)dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}(9 - 4y)dy &= [9y - 2y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= (9 - 2) - 0 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133489 1069 "e-12378" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133489 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 8xy \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt x \\right\\}$,\nand $D$ is the finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the line $x=1$, and the line $z=x$ in the first quadrant." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133489 1072 #uuid "72b1ac41-be39-5a51-bc0c-738d36cdb81b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133489 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 8xy \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}} 8xy\\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right ] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}8xydy]dA &= ∬_(D)4x[y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = \\sqrt{x}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4x(x - 0)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4x^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d102b63c3deb8121ea88cdc994b9410cf28f9c3bb29abc5ea4a14d7e1e7c389.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)4x^{2}dA &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x}4x^{2}dzdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}4x^{2}[z]_{z = 0}^{z = x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}4x^{2}(x - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}4x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= [x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= 1^{4} - 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133490 1069 "e-12398" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133490 1071 "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \\displaystyle \\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region defined as\n\n$R=\\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y}, \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq ye^{z+2} \\right\\}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133490 1072 #uuid "b51667a8-f48a-59a2-b09e-59c6fcad5507" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133490 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq u_2(y,z) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm{d}x \\right]\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(y,z) = -2, \\qquad u_2(y,z) = ye^{z+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{-2}^{ye^{z+2}} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -y^2 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dx]dA &= \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{-y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}[\\int_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dx]dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{-y^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\int_{-2}^{ye^{z + 2}}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133491 1069 "e-12400" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133491 1071 "![](images/b0/b0817c04ee57a8352f8f56a4c232668d415c1573e37a81729752d65c0fb9e1c1.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 36z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133491 1072 #uuid "21fdb746-8831-5b97-acf0-5a14a2e34980" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133491 1074 "![](images/05/0584c95ae22135288f08fc8d0ef472aae867eccecf958c65e82eb311da69a1ae.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 5-x-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/36/365451aa395e91cee59199b048ed59ce1fba2ee8ee569fc460ef8fe9bf3bcdfd.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 5-x-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 36z^2 \\: \\textrm{d}V = \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{5-x-z} 36z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{5 - x - z}36z^{2}dydzdx &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{5 - x - z}36z^{2}dy]dzdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}36z^{2}[y]_{y = 0}^{y = 5 - x - z}dzdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}36z^{2}(5 - x - z)dzdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(180z^{2} - 36xz^{2} - 36z^{3})dzdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(180z^{2} - 36xz^{2} - 36z^{3})dzdx &= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{1}(180z^{2} - 36xz^{2} - 36z^{3})dz]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[60z^{3} - 12xz^{3} - 9z^{4}]_{z = 0}^{z = 1}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(51 - 12x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}(51 - 12x)dx &= [51x - 6x^{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= (51(1) - 6(1)^{2}) - 0 \\\\\n&= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133492 1069 "e-12421" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133492 1071 "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2]$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133492 1072 #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133492 1074 "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad y = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad z = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi - xj + zk \\\\\n&= \\cos (πt)i - \\sin (πt)j + (2t - t^{2})k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin (πt))i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos (πt))j + \\frac{d}{dt}(2t - t^{2})k \\\\\n&= π\\cos (πt)i - π\\sin (πt)j + 2(1 - t)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (\\cos (πt)i - \\sin (πt)j + (2t - t^{2})k) \\cdot (π\\cos (πt)i - π\\sin (πt)j + 2(1 - t)k) \\\\\n&= \\cos (πt) \\cdot π\\cos (πt) + (-\\sin (πt)) \\cdot (- π\\sin (πt)) + (2t - t^{2}) \\cdot 2(1 - t) \\\\\n&= π\\cos^{2} (πt) + π\\sin^{2} (πt) + 2(2t - t^{2})(1 - t) \\\\\n&= π(\\cos^{2} (πt) + \\sin^{2} (πt)) + 2(2t - 3t^{2} + t^{3}) \\\\\n&= π + 4t - 6t^{2} + 2t^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ = ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr &= \\int_{0}^{2}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}π + 4t - 6t^{2} + 2t^{3}dt \\\\\n&= [πt + 2t^{2} - 2t^{3} + \\frac{1}{2}t^{4}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= [2π + 8 - 16 + 8] - [0] \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133493 1069 "e-12422" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133493 1071 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate\n$\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}$,\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle$, and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133493 1072 #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133493 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S$, then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $z=1$. One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation\n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) = 0 \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.curlF\\right|_{z = g(x, y)} &= ⟨ - xz,-yz, z^{2} + \\left.x⟩\\right|_{z = 1} \\\\\n&= ⟨- x_(⏟)_(P),-y_(⏟)_(Q), 1 + x_(⏟)_(R)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(- (-x) \\cdot 0 - (-y) \\cdot 0 + 1 + x)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(1 + x)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nof radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(1 + x)dA &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2π}(1 + r\\cos θ) \\cdot rdθdr \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2π}r + r^{2}\\cos θdθdr \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[rθ + r^{2}\\sin θ]_{0}^{2π}dr \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2πrdr \\\\\n&= π[r^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133494 1069 "e-12435" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133494 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1)$.\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k}$, evaluate the integral\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133494 1072 #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133494 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S$, then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad y = t^2-t, \\qquad z = t^2-t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= i + xj + yk \\\\\n&= i + (t^{2} - t^{3})j + (t^{2} - t)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t^{3})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)k \\\\\n&= (2t - 3t^{2})i + (2t - 1)j + (2t - 1)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (i + (t^{2} - t^{3})j + (t^{2} - t)k) \\cdot ((2t - 3t^{2})i + (2t - 1)j + (2t - 1)k) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (2t - 3t^{2}) + (t^{2} - t^{3}) \\cdot (2t - 1) + (t^{2} - t) \\cdot (2t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2t - 3t^{2} + (-t^{3} + 2t^{2} - t) \\cdot (2t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2t - 3t^{2} - 2t^{4} + t^{3} + 4t^{3} - 2t^{2} - 2t^{2} + t \\\\\n&= 3t - 7t^{2} + 5t^{3} - 2t^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS &= ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(3t - 7t^{2} + 5t^{3} - 2t^{4})dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{3}{2}t^{2} - \\frac{7}{3}t^{3} + \\frac{5}{4}t^{4} - \\frac{2}{5}t^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{7}{3} + \\frac{5}{4} - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{60}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133495 1069 "e-12447" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133495 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z)\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k}$, express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133495 1072 #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133495 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + z) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2}y) \\\\\n&= 1 + z + 0 \\\\\n&= z + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)z + 1dV &= \\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{y}^{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}z + 1dzdydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133496 1069 "e-12448" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133496 1071 "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}$. Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133496 1072 #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133496 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S$. Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∭_(R)3dV \\\\\n&= 3∭_(R)dV \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot Volume(R) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}π \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 32π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133497 1069 "e-12449" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133497 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=1$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k}$, find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133497 1072 #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133497 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4x^{2}yz) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2x^{2}y + 4x^{2}y \\\\\n&= 6x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S$. Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∭_(R)6x^{2}ydV \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}6x^{2}ydzdydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}3x^{2}dx \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}2ydy \\cdot \\int_{1}^{2}dz \\\\\n&= [x^{3}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\cdot [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2} \\cdot (2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (1 - 0) \\cdot (4 - 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133498 1069 "e-12768" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133498 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac{x-2}{x+1}$ for $x=5$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133498 1072 #uuid "e2e06b2a-d740-5280-addb-2796864dd5a8" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133498 1074 "We replace $x$ with $5$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x - 2}{x + 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5 - 2}{5 + 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{3}{6} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133499 1069 "e-12769" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133499 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac{1}{p} + \\dfrac{1}{2p}$ for $p=4$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133499 1072 #uuid "378a718e-c6cb-5b85-adc3-22ff4bd7bc9b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133499 1074 "We replace $p$ with $4$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{2p} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot 4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2 + 1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133500 1069 "e-12770" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133500 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac{3(2a-1)}{2(3a+1)}$ for $a=1$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133500 1072 #uuid "74f72809-7c28-5fc8-9e0e-b5542b5ffe4d" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133500 1074 "We replace $a$ with $1$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3(2a - 1))/(2(3a + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(3(2(1) - 1))/(2(3(1) + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(3(2 - 1))/(2(3 + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(3(1))/(2(4)) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133501 1069 "e-12771" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133501 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac{n-2}{m-1} + \\dfrac{2(m+1)}{n}$ for $m=4$ and $n=6$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133501 1072 #uuid "4e54a97f-16af-57c7-ab4e-c6f12e83306f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133501 1074 "We replace $m$ with $4$ and $n$ with $6$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n - 2}{m - 1} + (2(m + 1))/(n) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6 - 2}{4 - 1} + (2(4 + 1))/(6) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} + (2(5))/(6) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{10}{6} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{5}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4 + 5}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133502 1069 "e-12788" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133502 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $-21$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133502 1072 #uuid "fc3055a3-5118-5a5b-8150-caadbfdae02b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133502 1074 "To find the reciprocal of $-21$, we divide $1$ by $-21$. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n1 \\div (-21) = -\\dfrac{1}{21}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133503 1069 "e-12789" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133503 1071 "Find the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133503 1072 #uuid "2132b7c8-2d11-51c8-a1e3-06bb59c08f84" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133503 1074 "We flip the numerator and the denominator. So, the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10}{1} = 10\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133504 1069 "e-12790" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133504 1071 "Calculate the reciprocal of $-2\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133504 1072 #uuid "e757528d-e87f-5739-85d4-0b35267db955" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133504 1074 "First, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}=-\\dfrac{2\\cdot 3+1}{3}=-\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we flip the numerator and the denominator, keeping the negative sign. Therefore, the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133505 1069 "e-12791" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133505 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $0.4$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133505 1072 #uuid "7c2a7d91-c840-5f6a-8e9d-d17673e8cb70" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133505 1074 "First, we must convert the decimal to a fraction. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n0.4 = \\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}}{{\\color{red}{5}}}\n$$\n\nNow, we flip the fraction, as before. So the reciprocal of $0.4$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{5}}}{{\\color{blue}{2}}} = 2.5\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133506 1069 "e-12828" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133506 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$\\sqrt{2},\\qquad \\sqrt{8},\\qquad \\sqrt{16}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133506 1072 #uuid "0b823033-1f76-5656-a284-b94f33f3e359" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133506 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{2}=1.414\\,213\\,562\\,..$.\n- $\\sqrt{8}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{8}=2.828\\,427\\,124\\,..$.\n- $\\sqrt{16}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $16$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$) and obtain a whole number:\n$\\sqrt{16}=\\sqrt{4^2}=4$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{8}$ only." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133507 1069 "e-12829" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133507 1071 "What is the value of $\\sqrt{126}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133507 1072 #uuid "127ac025-aa32-505a-98d8-27721a37fdbe" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133507 1074 "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126}=11.224\\,972\\,160\\,..\n$$\n\nNow, we round to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126} \\approx 11.225\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133508 1069 "e-12830" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133508 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{2},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{11}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133508 1072 #uuid "9e145904-a9bf-59fc-9070-339b0e133de9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133508 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$, cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}}$, or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{\\phantom{0}}, \\sqrt[5]{\\phantom{0}},\\ldots$, etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1.259\\,921\\,\\ldots$\n- $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $27$ are perfect cubes, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}}|= \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1^{3}}{3^{3}}}; = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}}; = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}}; = \\sqrt[3]{(\\frac{1}{3})^{3}}; = \\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{11} = 2.223\\,980\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ only." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133509 1069 "e-12947" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133509 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is a square matrix\n2. $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n3. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133509 1072 #uuid "00b886e2-e15e-5b9e-a371-7c8e659febb1" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133509 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I$, our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133510 1069 "e-12948" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133510 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $\\mid \\textrm{det}(PQ) \\mid$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133510 1072 #uuid "b87bd2f0-67da-5b9d-9e21-831afa44eec8" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133510 1074 "We use the following facts:\n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1$.\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}det(PQ) & &= & det(P) \\cdot det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid (± 1) \\cdot det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}± 1 & \\cdot & det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid (-2\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 18 - 2 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 20 \\mid \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133511 1069 "e-12949" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133511 1071 "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133511 1072 #uuid "9d50c0e3-386e-57db-b509-bed0d933e6ef" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133511 1074 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD^{-1} &= D^{T} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133512 1069 "e-12950" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133512 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q$?\n\n1. $2Q$ is orthogonal\n2. $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n3. $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133512 1072 #uuid "848f3901-12b3-5a37-bb03-08743e7058c8" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133512 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q$, we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T$.\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T$, we obtain\n$2Q \\cdot (2Q)^{T}|= 2Q \\cdot 2Q^{T}; = 4QQ^{T}; = 4I; \\ne I$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T$, we obtain\n$(P^{-1}Q) \\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^{T}|= (P^{T}Q) \\cdot (P^{T}Q)^{T}; = P^{T}Q \\cdot Q^{T}(P^{T})^{T}; = P^{T}(QQ^{T})P; = P^{T}(I)P; = P^{T}P; = I$.\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T$, we obtain\n$(PQ^{T}) \\cdot (PQ^{T})^{T}|= PQ^{T} \\cdot (Q^{T})^{T}P^{T}; = PQ^{T}QP^{T}; = P(Q^{T}Q)P^{T}; = P(I)P^{T}; = PP^{T}; = I$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133513 1069 "e-12960" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133513 1071 "Calculate the value of $7^2$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133513 1072 #uuid "62c086b3-028f-528b-acd5-7bdc204dcb23" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133513 1074 "To work out $7^2$, we multiply $7$ by itself:\n\n$$\n7^2 = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133514 1069 "e-12961" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133514 1071 "Find the square of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133514 1072 #uuid "4a393145-3e63-532b-a288-775fbc7acd15" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133514 1074 "To work out\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwe multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nby itself:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^2 = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 = \\dfrac 1 4\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133515 1069 "e-12962" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133515 1071 "What is the value of $(-4)^2$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133515 1072 #uuid "55e8797b-62bd-5515-81a5-e1c13249c1cd" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133515 1074 "To work out $(-4)^2$, we multiply $-4$ by itself:\n\n$$\n(-4)^2 = (-4) \\cdot (-4) = 16\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133516 1069 "e-12964" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133516 1071 "What is the square of $-2.5$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133516 1072 #uuid "c07254e9-d6a6-55b1-bab7-4fe453c8cc69" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133516 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $(-2.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-2.5)^2 = (-2.5)\\cdot (-2.5) = (2.5)\\cdot (2.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ + & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0; 6 \\mid 2 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n25 \\times 25 = 625\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in the first factor ($2.5$) and $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in the second factor ($2.5$), so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $625$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2.5)^2 = 6.25\\,\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133517 1069 "e-13248" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133517 1071 "What is the value of $70^3$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133517 1072 #uuid "3e2f3025-cfc0-5688-a5be-dbe7f98848e3" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133517 1074 "To work out $70^3$, we multiply $70$ by itself and then multiply the result by $70$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n70^{3} &= 70 \\cdot 70 \\cdot 70 \\\\\n&= 4900 \\cdot 70 \\\\\n&= 343000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133518 1069 "e-13249" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133518 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\left(-13\\right)^3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133518 1072 #uuid "02fa99aa-811e-5d0a-b278-24ec7697a038" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133518 1074 "To work out\n\n$$\n\\left(-13\\right)^3\n$$\n\nwe multiply $-13$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-13$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-13)^{3} &= (-13) \\cdot (-13)^(⏞)^(169) \\cdot (-13) \\\\\n&= 169 \\cdot (-13) \\\\\n&=-2197\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133519 1069 "e-13251" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133519 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ cubed." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133519 1072 #uuid "5468caae-f88a-59d2-98ab-7941bf7cd81a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133519 1074 "To work out\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nwe multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nby itself and then multiply the result by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nagain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{3}{5})^{3} &= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{125}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133520 1069 "e-13253" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133520 1071 "What is the cube of $(-0.5)$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133520 1072 #uuid "ba7b4d05-42b6-58a5-9607-b30742991005" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133520 1074 "To work out\n\n$$\n\\left(-0.5\\right)^3\n$$\n\nwe multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-0.5)^{3} &= (-0.5) \\cdot (-0.5)^(⏞)^(0.25) \\cdot (-0.5) \\\\\n&= 0.25 \\cdot (-0.5) \\\\\n&=-0.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133521 1069 "e-13357" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133521 1071 "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133521 1072 #uuid "12b5f86f-9e86-5c1b-8773-38e2b69a6417" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133521 1074 "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}} = 0.645\\,497\\,\\ldots$\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n$\\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}}|= \\sqrt{\\frac{8^{2}}{9^{2}}}; = \\sqrt{\\frac{8 \\cdot 8}{9 \\cdot 9}}; = \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9} \\cdot \\frac{8}{9}}; = \\sqrt{(\\frac{8}{9})^{2}}; = \\frac{8}{9}$\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}} = 1.195\\,228\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}\n$$\n\nonly." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133522 1069 "e-13362" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133522 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${e^{-x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 0$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133522 1072 #uuid "38b4473a-f956-5af5-863b-387dc10b4924" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133522 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nthe cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-t}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-t}\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&=-e^{-x} + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ 1 - e^{-x},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133523 1069 "e-13363" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133523 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = e^{-\\mid 2x \\mid}, \\quad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133523 1072 #uuid "98dd834c-ac74-5cdd-88ee-060fd9ac689d" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133523 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{\\int_{- ∞}^{x}e^{2t}dt,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{- ∞}^{0}e^{2t}dt + \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-2t}dt,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x \\leq 0$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{x}e^{2t}dt|= \\left.\\frac{e^{2t}}{2}\\right|_{- ∞}^{x}; = \\frac{e^{2x}}{2}$,\nTherefore,\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}e^{2t}dt|= \\left.\\frac{e^{2x}}{2}\\right|_{x = 0}; = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- If $x > 0$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-2t}dt|= \\left.- \\frac{e^{-2t}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{x}; =-\\frac{e^{-2x}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}$.\nAs a result, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}e^{2t}dt + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-2t}dt|= \\frac{1}{2} + (-\\frac{e^{-2x}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}); = 1 - \\frac{e^{-2x}}{2}$.\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{e^{2x}}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ 1 - \\frac{e^{-2x}}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133524 1069 "e-13364" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133524 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{72}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 12, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133524 1072 #uuid "4159a407-ea9f-5728-b219-bf1afab732a2" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133524 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 12\n$$\n\nthe cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 12; 1, \\mid x > 12\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{72}tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{144}t^{2}\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{144}x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ \\frac{x^{2}}{144},\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 12; 1, \\mid x > 12\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133525 1069 "e-13365" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133525 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 5 \\\\ \\frac{x^{2} - 25}{75},\\end{vmatrix}5 \\le x \\le 10; 1, \\mid x > 10$,\n\nfind $P(2\\leq X \\leq 6)$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133525 1072 #uuid "707e3b49-9c13-50d8-8a7f-c4d5ec44e0ff" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133525 1074 "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 \\le X \\le 6) &= P(2 < X \\le 6) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 6) - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&= F(6) - F(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6^{2} - 25}{75} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{75}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133526 1069 "e-13447" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133526 1071 "Which of the following numbers are multiples of $3$?\n$6, \\quad 12, \\quad 16$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133526 1072 #uuid "b3697568-9cc4-5fad-9d61-b8d29d23a53e" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133526 1074 "Let's start by listing the first few multiples of $3$:\n\n$$\n3, \\quad 6, \\quad 9, \\quad 12, \\quad 15, \\quad 18, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- $6$ is a multiple of $3$.\n- $12$ is a multiple of $3$.\n- $16$ is *not* a multiple of $3$, since it does not appear in our list.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$6$ and $12$ only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133527 1069 "e-13449" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133527 1071 "Which of the following numbers are multiples of $35$?\n$70, \\quad 90, \\quad 105$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133527 1072 #uuid "d32ad4c9-62b4-573c-b305-5dca13fb7122" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133527 1074 "We start by drawing a number line. Then, we move along our number line in jumps of $35$, starting from zero:\n\n![](images/03/035904c1023f530546523bd2112a439193b2ea9798753a0cb480a763170f2d48.png)\n\nSo, the first few multiples of $35$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n35, \\quad 70, \\quad 105, \\quad 140, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- $70$ is a multiple of $35$.\n- $90$ is *not* a multiple of $35$, since it does not appear in our list.\n- $105$ is a multiple of $35$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$70$ and $105$ only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133528 1069 "e-13467" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133528 1071 "What is the sum of the first three positive multiples of $110$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133528 1072 #uuid "70bf5f0e-28d8-58a4-a7ad-511a6a8a9e47" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133528 1074 "To find the first three positive multiples of $110$, we multiply $110$ by $1$, $2$ and $3$, respectively:\n\n$$\n110 \\cdot 1 = 110; 110 \\cdot 2 = 110 + 110 = 220; 110 \\cdot 3 = 220 + 110 = 330\n$$\n\nSo, the sum is\n\n$$\n110+220+330 = 660\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133529 1069 "e-13468" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133529 1071 "List the first three positive multiples of $31$ in ascending order." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133529 1072 #uuid "042153be-e1a4-57ea-8f42-9d64ae9be48a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133529 1074 "To find the first three positive multiples of $31$, we multiply $31$ by $1$, $2$ and $3$, respectively:\n\n$$\n31 \\cdot 1 = 31; 31 \\cdot 2 = 31 + 31 = 62; 31 \\cdot 3 = 62 + 31 = 93\n$$\n\nSo, the first three positive multiples of $31$ are\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}31}, \\quad \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}62}, \\quad \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}93}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133530 1069 "e-1375" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133530 1071 "Evaluate $\\sqrt{x} + 2x -1$ when $x=16$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133530 1072 #uuid "6488c7bd-4179-5b78-bdfc-92973f9a141f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133530 1074 "We replace $x$ with $16$ in the given expression, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x} + 2x - 1 &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{16} + 2 \\cdot 16 - 1 &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4^{2}} + 32 - 1 &=  \\\\\n4 + 32 - 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133531 1069 "e-1377" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133531 1071 "Evaluate $\\sqrt{3x-1}$ for $x=3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133531 1072 #uuid "6c9e9059-c72a-5cd4-94ee-33cabb839cab" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133531 1074 "We replace $x$ with $3$ in the given expression, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3x - 1} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 3 - 1} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{9 - 1} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify even further using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{8} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 2} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{2^{2} \\cdot 2} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{2^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133532 1069 "e-1378" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133532 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{9x+3}}{\\sqrt{x-2}}$, find $f(5)$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133532 1072 #uuid "94bd4811-8dca-542d-8c23-1950c9212a3b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133532 1074 "We replace $x$ with $5$ in the given expression, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5) &= (\\sqrt{9(5) + 3})/(\\sqrt{(5) - 2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{48}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{48}{3}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133533 1069 "e-1379" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133533 1071 "Evaluate $\\sqrt[3]{x+6}$ at $x=2$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133533 1072 #uuid "5046e564-44ca-5e7b-bdbc-9388b5a30581" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133533 1074 "We replace $x$ with $2$ in the given expression, and simplify as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{x + 6} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{2 + 6} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{8} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{2^{3}} &=  \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133534 1069 "e-14325" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133534 1071 "What is the greatest common factor of $10y^2$ and $25y^4$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133534 1072 #uuid "1e23e6f6-e15a-5b2d-aa29-eb5c3e6e6291" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133534 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y^{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot y \\cdot y \\\\\n25y^{4} &= 5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y^{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5_(―) \\cdot y_(―) \\cdot y_(―) \\\\\n25y^{4} &= 5_(―) \\cdot 5 \\cdot y_(―) \\cdot y_(―) \\cdot y \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe greatest common factor is the product of these paired factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(10y^2, 25y^4) = {\\color{red}5} \\cdot {\\color{blue}y} \\cdot {\\color{green}y} = 5y^2\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133535 1069 "e-14330" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133535 1071 "What is the greatest common factor of $6x^2y^2$ and $3xy$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133535 1072 #uuid "9cee4a74-3618-513e-b5ec-ba7b1115a615" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133535 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x^{2}y^{2} &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot y \\cdot y \\\\\n3xy &= 3 \\cdot x \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x^{2}y^{2} &= 2 \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot x_(―) \\cdot x \\cdot y_(―) \\cdot y \\\\\n3xy &= 3_(―) \\cdot x_(―) \\cdot y_(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe greatest common factor is the product of these paired factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(6x^2y^2, 3xy) = {\\color{red}3} \\cdot {\\color{blue}x} \\cdot {\\color{green}y} = 3xy\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133536 1069 "e-14333" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133536 1071 "What is the greatest common factor of $-18p^2qr$ and $24p^3qr^2$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133536 1072 #uuid "07410525-71aa-598b-b85f-e043dc21ebee" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133536 1074 "The terms are $-18p^2qr$ and $24p^3qr^2$. When finding the greatest common factor, we ignore the negative sign.\n\nFirst, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18p^{2}qr &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot p \\cdot p \\cdot q \\cdot r \\\\\n24p^{3}qr^{2} &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot p \\cdot p \\cdot p \\cdot q \\cdot r \\cdot r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18p^{2}qr &= 2_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot p_(―) \\cdot p_(―) \\cdot q_(―) \\cdot r_(―) \\\\\n24p^{3}qr^{2} &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot p_(―) \\cdot p_(―) \\cdot p \\cdot q_(―) \\cdot r_(―) \\cdot r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe greatest common factor is the product of these paired factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(-18p^2qr, 24p^3qr^2) = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot {\\color{green}p} \\cdot {\\color{green}p} \\cdot {\\color{YellowOrange}q} \\cdot {\\color{Magenta}r} = 6p^2qr\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133537 1069 "e-14383" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133537 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is $a_n = 1-2n$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133537 1072 #uuid "00113d76-b950-513e-a3ea-29eef91c9cec" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133537 1074 "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n + d, \\qquad a_1 = \\_\\_\\_ \\,, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 1 - 2(1) =-1 \\\\\na_{2} &= 1 - 2(2) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&=-3 - (-1) \\\\\n&=-3 + 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}-2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1}=a_n{\\color{red}-2}, \\qquad a_1={\\color{blue}-1}, \\qquad n\\geq1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133538 1069 "e-14386" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133538 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is $f(n) = 3n-11$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133538 1072 #uuid "e04ad3e0-888e-51c0-b60c-e816b7df514b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133538 1074 "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + d, \\qquad f(1) = \\_\\_\\_ \\,, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 3(1) - 11 =-8 \\\\\nf(2) &= 3(2) - 11 =-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= f(2) - f(1) \\\\\n&=-5 - (-8) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)={\\color{blue}-8}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad f(1) = {\\color{blue}-8}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133539 1069 "e-14389" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133539 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence\n$a_{n+1} = a_n + 3,\\, \\qquad a_1 = -1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133539 1072 #uuid "d732cfb5-9db5-535d-adb1-5d3292f1c497" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133539 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1 = {\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\nand we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} + 3 \\\\\n&=-1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute\n\n$$\na_1 = {\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1+(n-1)d \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}-1} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}3}) \\\\[5pt] & =-1 + 3n - 3 \\\\[5pt] &=3n - 4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3n - 4, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133540 1069 "e-14393" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133540 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n$f(n+1) = f(n) - 5,\\, \\qquad f(1) = 3,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133540 1072 #uuid "26b3b89d-d485-51ac-b964-8377ce3aff28" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133540 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n) = f(1) + (n-1)d, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2) &= f(1) - 5 \\\\\n&= 3 - 5 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= f(2) - f(1) \\\\\n&=-2 - 3 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute\n\n$$\nf(1) = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}-5}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n)&= f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}3} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-5}) \\\\[5pt] & =3 - 5n + 5 \\\\[5pt] &=8 - 5n \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=8 - 5n, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133541 1069 "e-14739" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133541 1071 "What is the greatest common factor of $30ab^3$, $10a^3b$, and $15a^2b^2$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133541 1072 #uuid "c669c386-9aa8-5fcc-bdf0-d25cdaa1e32d" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133541 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30ab^{3} &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot a \\cdot b \\cdot b \\cdot b \\\\\n10a^{3}b &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot b \\\\\n15a^{2}b^{2} &= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot b \\cdot b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30ab^{3} &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5_(―) \\cdot a_(―) \\cdot b_(―) \\cdot b \\cdot b \\\\\n10a^{3}b &= 2 \\cdot 5_(―) \\cdot a_(―) \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot b_(―) \\\\\n15a^{2}b^{2} &= 3 \\cdot 5_(―) \\cdot a_(―) \\cdot a \\cdot b_(―) \\cdot b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe greatest common factor is the product of these paired factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}\\left(30ab^3, 10a^3b, 15a^2b^2\\right) = {\\color{red}5} \\cdot {\\color{blue}a} \\cdot {\\color{violet}b} = 5ab\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133542 1069 "e-1512" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133542 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n) =\\dfrac{6}{(-2)^n}$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133542 1072 #uuid "5537647b-d722-5000-8445-b2743cc1f1f8" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133542 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = r \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (6)/((-2)^{1}) = (6)/((-2)) =-3, \\\\\nf(2) &= (6)/((-2)^{2}) = \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = (f(2))/(f(1)) = ((\\frac{3}{2}))/((-3)) =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} f(n), \\qquad f(1) = -3, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133543 1069 "e-1513" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133543 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence\n$a_{n+1} = 3a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = 1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133543 1072 #uuid "19d183f9-cf05-5477-8da9-642e2fd8e03b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133543 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1=1$. To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = 3$.\n\nSubstituting $a_1 = 1$ and $r = 3$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 1 \\cdot 3^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133544 1069 "e-1604" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133544 1071 "At a grocery store, the price of a turkey is $N$ dollars. However, there is also a $7\\%$ sales tax.\n\nWhat expression represents the total cost of a turkey after tax is applied?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133544 1072 #uuid "93b2ed4a-45ad-5fc4-bfe8-479f5a674595" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133544 1074 "The price before taxes of the turkey is $N$ dollars.\n\nThe amount of tax is $7\\%$ of the price of the turkey, or $0.07N$.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost of the turkey after tax is:\n\n$$\nN+0.07N\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133545 1069 "e-19046" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133545 1071 "The temperature $T$ of a liquid, in degrees Fahrenheit, $t$ minutes after it starts cooling is given by\n\n$T(t) = 80 - 1.75t$.\nWhat will the temperature be after $24$ minutes?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133545 1072 #uuid "c77b02dd-086d-579b-8d08-a82767b180f3" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133545 1074 "We are told that the temperature is given by the function\n\n$$\nT(t) = 80 - 1.75t\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the number of minutes after cooling starts.\n\nWe substitute $t = 24$ into the temperature function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(24) &= 80 - 1.75(24) \\\\\n&= 80 - 42 \\\\\n&= 38\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the temperature will be $38^\\circ\\mathrm{F}$ after $24$ minutes." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133546 1069 "e-2584" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133546 1071 "Which numbered angle refers to the same angle as $\\widehat{GHA}$?\n\n![](images/47/47841a386b57f8615897cb73b1b98f15e975c78722f29d8baca92e7c7663a527.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133546 1072 #uuid "ebc8ba04-2c55-56bd-b07c-f5f95d0841c1" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133546 1074 "If we trace along the letters of the angle name, from $G$ to $H$ to $A$, we see that $\\widehat{GHA}$ refers to the angle $\\angle 3$.\n\n![](images/10/100349e33233f2152ce236be2d17dc2fe3a59035a88336e26554eb6864d6059a.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\angle{3}$ is another name for $\\widehat{GHA}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133547 1069 "e-2878" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133547 1071 "A stretch of factor $-2$ and invariant $y$-axis is applied to the point $(1,3)$. What is the resulting point?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133547 1072 #uuid "6b43e0a5-06d7-50d1-9888-5346d5fc6516" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133547 1074 "Applying a stretch of factor $-2$ and invariant $y$-axis to a point means that the $x$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $-2$.\n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,y\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is\n\n$$\n(1,3) \\mapsto \\left(-2\\cdot 1, 3\\right) = (-2,3)\n$$\n\n![](images/ad/ad7e4ca30140d363b340edfa904d98ea021232fd9c6cfc40efdaa8e7c9cdd8f4.png)\n\nNotice that the negative stretch factor has the effect of reflecting the point in the $y$-axis. So the point $(1,3)$ has been stretched by a factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction *and* reflected in the $y$-axis." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133548 1069 "e-2879" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133548 1071 "![](images/bc/bc6b64c74d76be6c803252d3bd95b8a395532fa5797d54edaff80e50c5afc5f5.png)\n\nThe polygon $\\mathcal{P}'$ is the image of the polygon $\\mathcal{P}$ under an enlargement with center $O$. What is the scale factor of the enlargement?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133548 1072 #uuid "a9344bad-8515-5ab0-b08b-4ece33ebf18f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133548 1074 "![](images/ac/ace1d66359e7f82f5cd952ac82fec973c63e8dc4231346f23eaaf33e39bfae85.png)\n\nNotice that, in the diagram above, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{B'C'}{BC} = \\dfrac52\n$$\n\nand the same proportion holds for the other corresponding sides too. As a result, the absolute value of the scale factor $k$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid k &= \\dfrac52\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the points $B$ and $B'$ lie in the same direction from $O$, the scale factor $k$ is positive. Therefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac52\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133549 1069 "e-2880" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133549 1071 "The image of the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ under the action of a stretch of factor $k$ and invariant $x$-axis is the segment $\\overline{P'Q'}$, as shown above. What is the value of $k$?\n\n![](images/be/be670f2b80e583add79a676c501bc3dc5e7f0a5b38e09a7f0382e78d5e3360ba.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133549 1072 #uuid "a5e4348b-6d0c-599c-8335-9034b84e35ad" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133549 1074 "Applying a stretch of factor $k$ and invariant $x$-axis to a point means that the $y$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $k$.\n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(x,ky\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the points $P(1,6)$ and $Q(9,8)$ are mapped to the points $P'(1,3)$ and $Q'(9,4)$, respectively, by the stretch, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1, 6) & ↦ (1, k \\cdot 6) = (1, 3), \\\\ (9, 8) & ↦ (9, k \\cdot 8) = (9, 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1, 6k) &= (1, 3), \\\\\n(9, 8k) &= (9, 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $y$-coordinates, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6k = 3 & ⇒k = \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 8k = 4 & ⇒k = \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133550 1069 "e-2883" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133550 1071 "![](images/7a/7a8d9e81fb71fab4596234c7b729340de8f39171eeb1493dc83876f62f4fbd0c.png)\n\nA stretch of factor $-2$ and invariant $y$-axis is applied to the polygon shown above. What is the resulting polygon?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133550 1072 #uuid "59f4a4ae-4f7e-588b-a651-4de094efcfa9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133550 1074 "Applying a stretch of factor $-2$ and invariant $y$-axis to a point means that the $x$-coordinate of the point should be multiplied by $-2$.\n\nThe following function represents this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,y\\right)\n$$\n\nTo stretch a polygon, we\n\n- find the images of the vertices, and then\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(-1,1)$, $(-3,2)$, $(-2,9)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n(-1, 1) ↦ (-2 \\cdot (-1), 1) = (2, 1); (-3, 2) ↦ (-2 \\cdot (-3), 2) = (6, 2); (-2, 9) ↦ (-2 \\cdot (-2), 9) = (4, 9)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/54/54b3d0ab08cf06a131721386aa5ad3097cf558b360804dc4f7fbf0a8babfaafe.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133551 1069 "e-2884" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133551 1071 "One of the polygons below is the image of the other polygon under a dilation. Which of the points shown is the center of the dilation?\n\n![](images/78/78560fcb843bb97f4f1462ec32e4bc82e2c86dc76cb39a09907205c7ba80af70.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133551 1072 #uuid "d183ccf4-9322-542a-9e8f-053803e5da06" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133551 1074 "First of all, notice that our figures have opposite orientations, which means that the scale factor of the dilation is negative.\n\nThe center of a dilation lies on any line which passes through a point and its image.\n\nTherefore, the center of our dilation can be found as, for example, the point of intersection of the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CC'}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/713f5211b38c2404db5e0077210c13664de8d954ec85ac66695bfeb0dde551e6.png)\n\nTherefore, the center of the dilation is the point $Q$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133552 1069 "e-2906" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133552 1071 "The quadrilateral $ABCD$ is rotated by an angle $\\alpha$ counterclockwise about the point $A$. The resulting polygon is $A'B'C'D'$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ coincides with $A'$\n2. $\\angle ACB \\cong \\angle A'C'B'$\n3. $AB = A'B'$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133552 1072 #uuid "25f331e5-db8f-58bd-a4a6-24d7ed09db10" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133552 1074 "The given transformation is a rotation of $\\alpha$ counterclockwise about the point $A$, which is a rigid motion. Rigid motions preserve the distances between all points and all angle measures.\n\n![](images/73/73d6148ffe8d269f7846cf95679cddef30e058380aef6546fb8bd7577d9e9b80.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Under the action of the transformation, the point $A$ is left unchanged. Therefore, $A=A'$.\n- Statement II is true. Under the action of the transformation, the measures of all corresponding angles are preserved. Therefore, $\\angle{ACB}\\cong \\angle{A'C'B'}$.\n- Statement III is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $AB = A'B'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133553 1069 "e-2907" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133553 1071 "Which of the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$, $\\mathcal{R}$, $\\mathcal{S}$ shown below can be obtained from the polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ with rigid motions?\n\n![](images/ab/abd2df7fd01c1d0b04bb9b25a2df48738532d12cab70c1d24038a018e04dd2db.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133553 1072 #uuid "46db11d7-8448-54f5-a5ea-46abfc746574" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133553 1074 "By transforming $\\mathcal{Q}$ using only rigid motions, we can get only polygons congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$.\n\nSimilarly, any polygon congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$ can be obtained from $\\mathcal{Q}$ using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{R}$ is not congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$, it can't be obtained from $\\mathcal{Q}$ using rigid motions.\n\nThe polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{S}$ are congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$. Therefore, we can obtain them by applying a combination of rigid motions to $\\mathcal{Q}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133554 1069 "e-2909" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133554 1071 "In terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ congruent?\n\n![](images/1d/1de244f4d4ad5ff86c6c300025edbc50e43415855487ae73e704478802e780e8.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133554 1072 #uuid "d928641d-fd70-5cbb-a217-e9e454a8bd51" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133554 1074 "The polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent because $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of rotation by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin.\n\n![](images/66/668fe5f1f5e0092a5ae6daceeada015ce6d84b25afea80570fea1aae318edc75.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133555 1069 "e-2912" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133555 1071 "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\n1. $(x,y) \\mapsto (x-3, y-4)$\n2. $(x,y) \\mapsto (2x, 2y)$\n3. $(x,y) \\mapsto (y, x)$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133555 1072 #uuid "5fb05996-774b-5a89-9cc1-6876cc60338e" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133555 1074 "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a translation of $3$ units to the left and $4$ units down. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n- Transformation II is a dilation of scale factor $2$. This is *not* a rigid motion. So, it does not map every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n- Transformation III is a reflection in the line $y=x$. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133556 1069 "e-4237" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133556 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $a_n = 8 \\cdot 3^n$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133556 1072 #uuid "6aa991c4-28e3-51fc-9642-19fa9346202d" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133556 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{1} = 8 \\cdot 3 = 24, \\\\\na_{2} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{2} = 8 \\cdot 9 = 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{72}{24} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3 a_n, \\quad a_1 = 24, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133557 1069 "e-4238" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133557 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n$f(n+1) =-\\dfrac{f(n)}{3},\\, \\quad f(1) = {\\color{blue}9},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133557 1072 #uuid "5027a265-fd62-574b-b6fa-2bacf9de7304" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133557 1074 "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n + 1) = - (f(n))/(3) =-\\frac{1}{3}f(n)\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n) =f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)=9\n$$\n\nTo find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = {\\color{red}r} \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf(1) = 9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n) &= f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 9 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n) &= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{-1} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{1}) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&=-27 \\cdot (1)/((-3)^{n}) \\\\\n&= - (27)/((-3)^{n})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\dfrac{27}{(-3)^n},\\quad n\\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133558 1069 "e-4732" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133558 1071 "Consider $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$.\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A$, is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133558 1072 #uuid "126c85b8-d721-5e7d-a3dc-df997bc0b9ce" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133558 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the second row of $I$ and adding $7$ times the third row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 7R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary\nrow operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+7R_3\n$$\n\nto $A$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133559 1069 "e-4733" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133559 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation $R_2:=R_2-2R_1$ to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$. What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133559 1072 #uuid "f8eb89c4-1cca-5f8c-909c-31e9e0fc8412" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133559 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2-2R_1\n$$\n\nto $A$.\n\nTo find $E$, we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133560 1069 "e-4734" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133560 1071 "Suppose we are applying these two elementary row operations, in the given order, to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$:\n$R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3, \\qquad R_1:=6R_1$\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133560 1072 #uuid "6e47a532-ba7a-5692-961b-eabc743d74e6" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133560 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3$. The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n$I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid ↔R_{3}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; = E_{1}$\n- The second operation is $R_1:=6R_1$. The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n$I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=6R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = E_{2}$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A$, we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A$, we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133561 1069 "e-4861" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133561 1071 "Which of the following numbers are irrational?\n\n1. $\\sqrt 3$\n2. $\\dfrac32$\n3. $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt2} 2$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133561 1072 #uuid "1e830784-49d8-5cc7-9703-d1a7513333e9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133561 1074 "First, recall the following:\n\n- A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n- Any real number is irrational if it is not rational.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt 3$ is an irrational number. It cannot be expressed as the ratio of two integers.\n- $\\dfrac32$ is not an irrational number. It is a rational number.\n- $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ is an irrational number. It cannot be expressed as the ratio of two integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133562 1069 "e-5241" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133562 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-5} = \\sqrt{4 - x}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133562 1072 #uuid "44279c6a-3415-5a1b-aff5-35de63531d6e" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133562 1074 "To solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-5} = \\sqrt{4 - x}\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x - 5} &= \\sqrt{4 - x} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x - 5})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{4 - x})^{2} \\\\\n2x - 5 &= 4 - x \\\\\n3x &= 9 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we check that the obtained value $x=3$ is actually a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2(3) - 5} &= ^(?)\\sqrt{4 - 3} \\\\\n\\sqrt{1} &= ^(?)\\sqrt{1} \\\\\n1 &= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-5} = \\sqrt{4 - x}\n$$\n\nis $x=3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133563 1069 "e-5243" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133563 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt {x - 2} = \\sqrt {3x} - 2$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133563 1072 #uuid "63385981-813e-56cb-a295-cc7dd5a0f874" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133563 1074 "Even though we have an extra term on the right-hand side, we proceed in the same way and raise both sides of the equation to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x - 2} &= \\sqrt{3x} - 2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{x - 2})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{3x} - 2)^{2} \\\\\nx - 2 &= (\\sqrt{3x})^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3x} \\cdot 2 + 2^{2} \\\\\nx - 2 &= 3x - 4\\sqrt{3x} + 4 \\\\\n4\\sqrt{3x} &= 3x - x + 4 + 2 \\\\\n4\\sqrt{3x} &= 2x + 6 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{3x} &= x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have an equation with one square root on one side and a linear expression on the other side. To solve this type of equation, we raise both sides to the power of $2$ one more time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{3x})^{2} &= (x + 3)^{2} \\\\\n2^{2} \\cdot 3x &= (x)^{2} + 2(x) \\cdot 3 + 3^{2} \\\\\n12x &= x^{2} + 6x + 9 \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} + 6x + 9 - 12x \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} - 6x + 9 \\\\\n0 &= (x - 3)^{2} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we check that the obtained value $x=3$ is actually a solution to the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3 - 2} &= ^(?)\\sqrt{3(3)} - 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{1} &= ^(?)3 - 2 \\\\\n1 &= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {x - 2} = \\sqrt {3x} - 2\n$$\n\nhas a single solution $x=3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133564 1069 "e-5244" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133564 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\sqrt {2x +5} -\\sqrt {x+6} = 1$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133564 1072 #uuid "1180aec5-0222-5c7b-9449-1e7b636b35f7" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133564 1074 "First, we move the radicals to separate sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} - \\sqrt{x + 6} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we raise both sides to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x + 5})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1)^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= (\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= x + 7 + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= 2x + 5 - x - 7 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have an equation with one square root on one side and a linear expression on the other side. To solve this type of equation, we raise both sides to the power of $2$ one more time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\n4(x + 6) &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\n4x + 24 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 8x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 2x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 10)(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 10,-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we check the obtained values by substituting them into the original equation.\n\n- Checking $x=10$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(10) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(10) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16} + 1; 5|= ^(?)4 + 1; 5|= 5.✓$\nSo, the value $x=10$ is indeed a solution of the original equation\n- Checking $x=-2$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(-2) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-2) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{1} = 1 \\mid \\ne 2 + 1 = 3.\\cdot$\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {2x +5} -\\sqrt {x+6} = 1\n$$\n\nhas a single solution $x=10$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133565 1069 "e-5389" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133565 1071 "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133565 1072 #uuid "abb40124-5eda-57b4-a815-9cd8b5661da1" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133565 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwith the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133566 1069 "e-5390" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133566 1071 "![](images/24/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133566 1072 #uuid "6272bafb-8e57-583f-a320-f066fe40184b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133566 1074 "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133567 1069 "e-5391" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133567 1071 "![](images/1e/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133567 1072 #uuid "04f489ae-768f-5e8e-bed6-0a88f481c54c" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133567 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133568 1069 "e-5392" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133568 1071 "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P$, as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?\n\n![](images/72/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133568 1072 #uuid "e2c565be-0152-5088-8c4f-e66374cf2fb9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133568 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 7\\cos (\\frac{4π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 7\\sin (\\frac{4π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right)\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133569 1069 "e-5436" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133569 1071 "The linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ has the standard matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat are the images of the standard basis vectors $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133569 1072 #uuid "4748324b-8edf-5fba-90e1-90683887d463" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133569 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ are the first and second columns of $T$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(j) &= T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot\\mathbf{i}$ and $T\\cdot\\mathbf{j}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= T \\cdot i = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(j) &= T \\cdot j = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133570 1069 "e-5437" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133570 1071 "In the diagram below, $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, while $\\mathbf{i'}$ and $\\mathbf{j'}$ are their images under the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$.\nWhat is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n![](images/4d/4d1b9e56c0c6214d949d12f45fe4aecea5516971203724ecf573a2b17c6e959a.png)" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133570 1072 #uuid "0db64243-ffa4-595b-8555-5ee369450557" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133570 1074 "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{i}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n- the image of $\\mathbf{j}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133571 1069 "e-5438" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133571 1071 "Given that $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}, \\mathbf k\\}$ is the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the images of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$ under the action of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ are\n$\\langle 3, 5, -1 \\rangle$, $\\langle 0, 4,3 \\rangle$, and $\\langle -1, 5, -2\\rangle$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133571 1072 #uuid "ae7884f7-abb9-56ba-b685-f0eab27ff795" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133571 1074 "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133572 1069 "e-5536" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133572 1071 "Find $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$, if $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2$, $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} = -1$, $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 2$, and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}=3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133572 1072 #uuid "c374456a-bff7-5145-a590-620bbf50dabf" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133572 1074 "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5a + 3b) \\cdot c + (4b + 2c) \\cdot d &= (5a) \\cdot c + (3b) \\cdot c + (4b) \\cdot d + (2c) \\cdot d \\\\\n&= 5(a \\cdot c) + 3(b \\cdot c) + 4(b \\cdot d) + 2(c \\cdot d)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can substitute the values for $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$, and use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(a \\cdot c) + 3(b \\cdot c) + 4(b \\cdot d) + 2(c \\cdot d) &= 5(c \\cdot a) + 3(-1) + 4(2) + 2(d \\cdot c) \\\\\n&= 5(c \\cdot a) - 3 + 8 + 2(d \\cdot c) \\\\\n&= 5(c \\cdot a) + 5 + 2(d \\cdot c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(c \\cdot a) + 5 + 2(d \\cdot c) &= 5(2) + 5 + 2(3) \\\\\n&= 10 + 5 + 6 \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 21\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133573 1069 "e-5537" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133573 1071 "Suppose $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$, where $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = -2(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a})$\n2. $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 0$\n3. $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133573 1072 #uuid "13909d3e-b3df-5fcb-9056-5d71283fbebe" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133573 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Simplifying, we have\n$(4a + 2b) \\cdot a|= 0; (4a) \\cdot a + (2b) \\cdot a|= 0; 4(a \\cdot a) + 2(b \\cdot a)|= 0; 2(b \\cdot a)|=-4(a \\cdot a); b \\cdot a|=-2(a \\cdot a)$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$, we must have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0$.\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. Simplifying $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$ and substituting in $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$, we get\n$(4a + 2b) \\cdot a|= 0; 4a \\cdot a + 2b \\cdot a|= 0; 4a \\cdot a + 2(1)|= 0; a \\cdot a|=-\\frac{1}{2}$,\nbut this is a contradiction since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ implies that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133574 1069 "e-5538" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133574 1071 "Given $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133574 1072 #uuid "0ab996ae-e3a7-5353-87c2-9b2a01f4f808" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133574 1074 "Using the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖x‖ &= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + (-4)^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 16 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{50} \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133575 1069 "e-5539" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133575 1071 "Given the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ find the distance $\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y})$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133575 1072 #uuid "f2ff1620-28e1-59ba-97ca-c43f3f300fdf" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133575 1074 "Using the formula for the distance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, y) &= ‖x - y‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - y_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - y_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - y_{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 0)^{2} + (3 - (-1))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133576 1069 "e-5540" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133576 1071 "Normalize the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133576 1072 #uuid "7b7adf29-1653-5de7-915c-80a09f05995a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133576 1074 "To normalize a vector $\\mathbf{x}$, we have to divide the vector by its norm:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to normalize $\\mathbf{x}$, we first calculate $\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖x‖ &= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-8)^{2} + 6^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{64 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $\\mathbf{x}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖} &= \\frac{x}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-8}{10} \\\\ \\frac{6}{10}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133577 1069 "e-5542" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133577 1071 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 7 x_3 y_3$. Find $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133577 1072 #uuid "d8ab385f-ca7e-5ac2-9c0c-23ec78c9b1f7" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133577 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 3x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 7x_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-3) + (-2) \\cdot 1 + 7 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-9 - 2 + 0 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133578 1069 "e-5543" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133578 1071 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nFind $\\langle \\mathbf{x}(t), \\mathbf{y}(t) \\rangle$, if\n${x}(t) = 1-2t+3t^2, \\quad {y}(t) = t-t^2$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133578 1072 #uuid "d7217dd1-5e4d-5ecb-96a4-e4c7064f2f88" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133578 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0 + 9 \\cdot (-2) + 22 \\cdot (-6) \\\\\n&= 0 - 18 - 132 \\\\\n&=-150\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133579 1069 "e-5544" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133579 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$, the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$. Find $\\langle \\sin t, \\cos t \\rangle$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133579 1072 #uuid "007b171e-6587-5835-a59c-443b29c2b667" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133579 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle \\sin t, \\cos t \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\sin{t} \\cos{t} \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin2t =2\\sin t\\cos t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin t\\cos tdt &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin 2tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.- \\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2t\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}(\\cos 4π - \\cos 0) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133580 1069 "e-5545" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133580 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133580 1072 #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133580 1074 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= x_{1}y_{1}^(―) + x_{2}y_{2}^(―) + x_{3}y_{3}^(―) \\\\\n&= \\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3^(―)) + (-2) \\cdot 4 + \\textrm{i}^(―) + 0 \\cdot 5^(―) \\\\\n&= \\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3) + (-2) \\cdot (4 - \\textrm{i}) + 0 \\\\\n&=-3\\textrm{i} - 8 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&=-8 - \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133581 1069 "e-5547" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133581 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n2. $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n3. $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133581 1072 #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133581 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}$.\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n$\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\,} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},-\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$\nfor any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n$\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{\\mathrm{i}}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$\nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$. But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133582 1069 "e-5548" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133582 1071 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 1 - t^2$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133582 1072 #uuid "105a5a4a-2fea-54bb-97c6-c2a700b05f61" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133582 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖ = \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n{p}(t) = 1-t^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad p(2)= -3,\\qquad p(3)=-8\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖p(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{p(1)p(1) + p(2)p(2) + p(3)p(3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2} + [p(2)]^{2} + [p(3)]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(0)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (-8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 9 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{73}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133583 1069 "e-5549" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133583 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133583 1072 #uuid "5aafdd7d-939a-5690-ac4d-46b554524d75" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133583 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(2t - 1)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(4t^{2} - 4t + 1)dt \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{4t^{3}}{3} - 2t^{2} + t)\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} - 2 + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133584 1069 "e-5550" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133584 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133584 1072 #uuid "d279bb1d-754e-5044-9a01-d4c86f30b269" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133584 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{π}(3t)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}9t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.3t^{3}\\right|_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 3π^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\| = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t)$, we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= (f(t))/(‖f(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π\\sqrt{3π}} \\cdot 3t \\\\\n&= \\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{π\\sqrt{π}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133585 1069 "e-5551" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133585 1071 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133585 1072 #uuid "7aec717f-6f52-590d-9cd0-47c34b587e1c" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133585 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-4) + (-3) \\cdot 6 + k \\cdot (-6) + (-2) \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&=-20 - 18 - 6k - 16 \\\\\n&=-6k - 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6k - 54 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6k &= 54 \\\\\nk &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133586 1069 "e-5552" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133586 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w})$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133586 1072 #uuid "ce2ff5a8-84bb-5d2b-8078-623203f1a3b5" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133586 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0\n$$\n\n. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u + v) \\cdot w + u \\cdot (v + w) &= u \\cdot w + v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v + u \\cdot w \\\\\n&= 0 + v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v + 0 \\\\\n&= v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (-1) + (-3) \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= + ((-1) \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot (-3) + 5 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= (-2 - 9 + 4) + (-2 + 6 + 20) \\\\\n&=-7 + 24 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133587 1069 "e-5553" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133587 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133587 1072 #uuid "8b0122e7-3500-50cf-8505-22970428dac6" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133587 1074 "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n{u \\cdot v = 0; u \\cdot w = 0\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0\n$$\n\n. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= 0 \\\\\n- 8 \\cdot 6 + a \\cdot (-15) + b \\cdot 9 + (-10) \\cdot (-12) &= 0 \\\\\n- 48 - 15a + 9b + 120 &= 0 \\\\\n- 15a + 9b &= -72 \\\\\n- 5a + 3b &= -24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot w &= 0 \\\\\n- 8 \\cdot 4 + a \\cdot 2 + b \\cdot (-5) + (-10) \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\n- 32 + 2a - 5b + 30 &= 0 \\\\\n2a - 5b &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{-5a + 3b =-24; 2a - 5b = 2\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n{-10a + 6b =-48; 10a - 25b = 10\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - 5(2) &= 2 \\\\\n2a - 10 &= 2 \\\\\n2a &= 12 \\\\\na &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133588 1069 "e-5554" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133588 1071 "Consider the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Given that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=-12$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v}\\Vert =4$, what is the smallest possible value of $k$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133588 1072 #uuid "77b63ba5-410b-536c-8ed0-92c895097cc9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133588 1074 "We will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality,\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert\n$$\n\nWe are given all the quantities in the inequality above, except for $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$. So first, we compute $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (\\sqrt{k})^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6 + k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid u \\cdot v \\mid \\mid \\le ‖u‖ \\cdot ‖v‖; \\mid - 12 \\mid \\mid \\le \\sqrt{6 + k} \\cdot (4); 12\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 4\\sqrt{6 + k} \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}\\le \\sqrt{6 + k}; 3^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le (\\sqrt{6 + k})^{2} \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}\\le 6 + k; 3 \\mid \\le k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $k$ is $3$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133589 1069 "e-5555" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133589 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133589 1072 #uuid "763f7e8e-081a-57ad-825f-601a38fe3984" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133589 1074 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert \\, \\Vert \\mathbf{w}\\Vert}\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{v_{1}w_{1} + v_{2}w_{2} + v_{3}w_{3} + v_{4}w_{4}}{\\sqrt{v_{1}^{2} + v_{2}^{2} + v_{3}^{2} + v_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{w_{1}^{2} + w_{2}^{2} + w_{3}^{2} + w_{4}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= ((-1) \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 2 + (-4) \\cdot (-2) + (-2) \\cdot 2)/(\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + 2^{2} + (-4)^{2} + (-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + 2^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{25} \\cdot \\sqrt{16}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{5 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133590 1069 "e-5557" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133590 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors such that $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 6$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+4\\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 25$. If $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$, find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133590 1072 #uuid "0c2486d6-bb55-52ba-9fd8-6b516d3f7858" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133590 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$, we also have that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $4\\mathbf{v}$. So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + ‖4v‖^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + (4‖v‖)^{2} \\\\\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + 16‖v‖^{2} \\\\\n(25)^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + 16(6)^{2} \\\\\n625 &= ‖u‖^{2} + 576 \\\\\n‖u‖^{2} &= 625 - 576 \\\\\n‖u‖^{2} &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=\\Vert\\mathbf{u}\\Vert^2=49\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133591 1069 "e-5558" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133591 1071 "Given that $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} +3\\mathbf{v} \\Vert=14$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert=2$, find the smallest possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}\\Vert$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133591 1072 #uuid "112253ae-e0ea-554a-83bf-87f8ba7c9ce7" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133591 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n‖u + 3v‖ \\mid \\le ‖u‖ + ‖3v‖\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}‖u + 3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖ + 3‖v‖ \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖ + 3(2) \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖ + 6 \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖u‖\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ is $8$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133592 1069 "e-5559" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133592 1071 "Let the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be orthogonal.\nIf $\\mid \\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\left.\\mathbf{v}\\right|^2+5\\Vert\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\Vert^2=29$ and $\\| \\mathbf{u} \\| = \\| \\mathbf{w} \\| = 2$, then what is the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133592 1072 #uuid "5e72235b-a419-5f27-bee7-01be8b49c997" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133592 1074 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n29 &= \\mid u \\cdot \\left.v\\right|^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&\\le ‖u‖^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&= 4‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} &= 4‖v‖^{2} + 5(‖v‖^{2} + ‖w‖^{2}) \\\\\n&\\le 4‖v‖^{2} + 5(‖v‖^{2} + 2^{2}) \\\\\n&= 9‖v‖^{2} + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}29 & \\le 9‖v‖^{2} + 20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v‖^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v‖^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2\n$$\n\nimplies that\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert\n$$\n\n. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133593 1069 "e-5560" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133593 1071 "For the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133593 1072 #uuid "bbb751a9-ef0d-58cf-b788-18594dd67337" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133593 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.\n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set.\n\n- For the set $H$:\n$u \\cdot h_{1}|= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-6) + 6 \\cdot 1 = 0, ✓; u \\cdot h_{2}|= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-3) + 6 \\cdot (-1) =-6 \\ne 0\\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2$. Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H$, we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H$.\n\n- For the set $V$:\n$u \\cdot v_{1}|= 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}) = 0✓; u \\cdot v_{2}|= 3 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 3 + 6 \\cdot 0 = 0✓$\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2$. Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V$, we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133594 1069 "e-5561" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133594 1071 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133594 1072 #uuid "bbed6e9e-f63e-599f-ab8e-71cff46cfcfd" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133594 1074 "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{u \\cdot w_{1} = 0; u \\cdot w_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3$. So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{3} \\\\ -x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_3=1$, we get the vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3$, $b=-1$, and $c=1$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133595 1069 "e-5562" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133595 1071 "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133595 1072 #uuid "ef531549-f5be-5002-b94f-1c7ee8682784" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133595 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2$.\n\nA vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{x \\cdot h_{1} = 0; x \\cdot h_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - \\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get\n\n$$\nx_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133596 1069 "e-5563" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133596 1071 "Determine whether the following set is orthonormal:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133596 1072 #uuid "4b092f86-cf53-51b4-9f79-b43d58c8b2e5" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133596 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given set as $\\{\\mathbf{w}_1,\\mathbf{w}_2\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe set $W$ is orthonormal if the following conditions hold:\n\n$$\n{w_{1} \\cdot w_{1} = 1; w_{2} \\cdot w_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our set:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\cdot w_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} = 0 & ✓ \\\\ w_{1} \\cdot w_{1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 1 & ✓ \\\\ w_{2} \\cdot w_{2}|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} = 16 + 16 = 32 \\ne 1 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are orthogonal, and $\\mathbf w_1$ is a unit vector, but $\\mathbf w_2$ is *not* a unit vector.\n\nTherefore, ${W}$ is *not* an orthonormal set." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133597 1069 "e-5564" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133597 1071 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T$, extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133597 1072 #uuid "af1ca39e-5cb7-5a93-b88f-0ff86a563bc6" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133597 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2$. To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$, we need to find a third vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n{u_{1} \\cdot x = 0; u_{2} \\cdot x = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). This means $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_3=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-x_{1} - x_{2} + 2(0) = 0⟹x_{1} =-x_{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $x_2=1$, we obtain the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133598 1069 "e-5570" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133598 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133598 1072 #uuid "1ad2672a-5773-5f2e-ab81-64e51ab4a7e2" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133598 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{b \\cdot a}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 9 + 0 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2} + 1^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 9 + 0 - 1}{4 + 1 + 0 + 1}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{6}b \\\\\n&= 2b \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133599 1069 "e-5571" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133599 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ If $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{z}$, where $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, then find $\\mathbf{z}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133599 1072 #uuid "2031cdd7-c43e-509c-8bb2-4d7362914e7b" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133599 1074 "A diagram of the situation is provided below:\n\n![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a}x &= \\frac{a \\cdot x}{a \\cdot a}a \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot 4 + (-1) \\cdot (-4) + 0 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 2)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2})a \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 4 + 0 - 2}{1 + 1 + 0 + 1}a \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{3}a \\\\\n&= 2a \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= proj_{a}x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nz &= x - proj_{a}x = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, as shown in the picture above." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133600 1069 "e-5572" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133600 1071 "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133600 1072 #uuid "5168f07e-4a22-5e0c-a612-e5c6f77f99eb" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133600 1074 "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get $x_2=4x_3$.\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1+4x_3+4x_3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=-8x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsequently, $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= ((-10) \\cdot (-8) + 20 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 1)/((-8)^{2} + 4^{2} + 1^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{80 + 80 + 2}{64 + 16 + 1}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{162}{81}b \\\\\n&= 2b \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133601 1069 "e-5573" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133601 1071 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133601 1072 #uuid "89a4773c-f6bb-577d-8743-5d0a46c1da6d" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133601 1074 "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a_{1}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{1}}{a_{1} \\cdot a_{1}}a_{1} = (2 \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1)/(1^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2})a_{1} = 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{a_{2}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{2}}{a_{2} \\cdot a_{2}}a_{2} = (2 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1)/((-2)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2})a_{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{a_{1}}x + proj_{a_{2}}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + (-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133602 1069 "e-5574" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133602 1071 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133602 1072 #uuid "4985be13-9b44-5ec5-b342-00354339b902" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133602 1074 "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a_{1}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{1}}{a_{1} \\cdot a_{1}}a_{1} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 1}{1^{2} + 2^{2} + 1^{2}}a_{1} = \\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{a_{2}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{2}}{a_{2} \\cdot a_{2}}a_{2} = (3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2})a_{2} = \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{a_{1}}x + proj_{a_{2}}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = x^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, x^(^)) &= ‖x - x^(^)‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x^(^)_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - x^(^)_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - x^(^)_{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 2)^{2} + (1 - 2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133603 1069 "e-5575" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133603 1071 "Verify whether the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133603 1072 #uuid "b8bbe0e1-8bf1-5b3a-a77e-6350ea77e83f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133603 1074 "First, we verify whether $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3}↔R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}26 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 26\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133604 1069 "e-5576" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133604 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}$, where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133604 1072 #uuid "ac8363a8-fc77-52e0-b060-5e3a45c5171a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133604 1074 "First, notice that $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ are linearly independent.\n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A\\left(A^T\\!A\\right)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace\n\n$$\nS=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$. In our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; v \\mid w; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{32}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133605 1069 "e-5577" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133605 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133605 1072 #uuid "a348815b-cc51-569b-bcc7-d46ccc40fc68" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133605 1074 "Recall that the matrix\n\n$$\nP_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\n$$\n\nmaps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A$.\n\nIn our case, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (14)^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133606 1069 "e-5578" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133606 1071 "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133606 1072 #uuid "5f72c4ca-3bd8-5398-8dc4-ee790127f844" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133606 1074 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if $Q^2=Q$ and $Q^T=Q$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$A^T = A$\nbut\n$A^{2}|= A \\cdot A; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne A$.\n- $B$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$B^{T} \\ne B$.\n- $C$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n$C^T = C$\nand\n$C^{2}|= C \\cdot C; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = C$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133607 1069 "e-5580" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133607 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ above spans $S^\\perp$, and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{13} + p_{32}$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133607 1072 #uuid "ac4c3c85-2831-57b2-bef7-5be9c61ea365" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133607 1074 "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto\n\n$$\nS^\\perp = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\nP_{S^\\perp} = \\mathbf{a} (\\mathbf{a}^T\\!\\mathbf{a})^{-1} \\mathbf{a}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S^{⊥}} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\nP_S + P_{S^\\perp} = I\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= I - P_{S^{⊥}} \\\\\n&= I - \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np_{13} + p_{32} = (-2) + (-2) = -4\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133608 1069 "e-5581" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133608 1071 "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$, given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133608 1072 #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133608 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1}$.\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - ((-3) \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot (-1) + 2 \\cdot 2)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 2^{2})v_{1}; = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac 7 3\n$$\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 tru