5585 true] [17592186133610 1072 #uuid "60dbc829-01fc-5283-8741-68af483d5313" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133610 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n$v_{2}|= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1}$.\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n$= a_{2} - (6 \\cdot 2 + 0 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 2^{2} + (-1)^{2})v_{1}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis an orthonormal basis for $V$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n$$\nb=-\\dfrac1 3\n$$\n\n$$\nc=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & + & b & + & c\\end{vmatrix} = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133611 1069 "e-5584" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133611 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}$. Given that $\\mathcal B = \\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf b\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V, \\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}$?" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133611 1072 #uuid "683556db-c65d-5279-ac14-7dfaa496db0a" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133611 1074 "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - (-\\frac{2}{4})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{0}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - 0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\n$$\nc=-\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133612 1069 "e-5585" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133612 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}$. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfr