ligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nq_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133614 1069 "e-5588" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133614 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n${-2x_{1} - x_{2} = 12; x_{1} - 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 0; - x_{2} - 2x_{3} =-12; x_{1} - x_{3} = 0$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133614 1072 #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133614 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133615 1069 "e-5589" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133615 1071 "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133615 1072 #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133615 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133616 1069 "e-5590" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133616 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133616 1072 #uuid "cb31a55d-6107-509a-80e2-9bdffcaff6e3" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133616 1074 "The least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} + 3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133617 1069 "e-5591" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133617 1071 "${-x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 1; 2x_{1} - 8x_{2} = 1; - 2x_{1} + 8x_{2} =-1$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b$." 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133617 1072 #uuid "c5097abf-c541-5c52-85d3-1e56a4e53cb9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133617 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 4R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} + 4x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133618 1069 "e-5592" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133618 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR$, find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133618 1072 #uuid "5fb2571c-2ab9-5fe5-9454-f9d7b0e89854" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133618 1074 "The least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax &= A^{T}b \\\\\n(QR)^{T}QRx &= (QR)^{T}b \\\\\nR^{T}(Q^{T}Q)Rx &= R^{T}Q^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I$, and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R^{T})^{-1} \\cdot R^{T}Rx &= (R^{T})^{-1} \\cdot R^{T}Q^{T}b \\\\\nRx &= Q^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)x &= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(Q^{T})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{7x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 28; 2x_{2} = 14\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x_{1} - 3(7) &= 28 \\\\\n7x_{1} &= 49 \\\\\nx_{1} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133619 1069 "e-5610" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133619 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133619 1072 #uuid "251ec7c6-a050-5deb-8f13-d81f5de4b5c9" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133619 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T$, we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X)^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ^(^) &= (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny = 6.75 - x\n$$" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133620 1069 "e-5611" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133620 1071 "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133620 1072 #uuid "a763a84a-0727-58a3-b524-04afa5735706" 13194139533360 17592186045585 true] [17592186133620 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T$, we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X)^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ^(^) &= (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line\n\n$$\ny = 0.04+ 0.82x\n$$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.04 + 0.82(8) \\\\\n&= 6.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$